ge(1), CpuTemp(1)?? Hmm if temp shown separately it's extra block. In image CPU block is \"88% (28°C)\" inside one block. Let's model a \"combo\": MetricKind.CpuUsage renders as `88%` plus optional temp in parentheses if CpuTemp metric enabled in same row & adjacent? Too magic.\n\nBetter: single metric \"CPU\" with flags `ShowPercent`, `ShowTemperature`. Similarly GPU, VRAM. I'll define kinds:\n- Cpu (percent + temp + label \"CPU\")\n- Gpu (percent + temp + label \"GPU\")\n- Ram (used/total + optional percent)\n- Vram (used/total + optional percent + temp)\n- NetUp, NetDown\n- Disk (activity + free space)\n- Battery (percent + AC)\n\nThis matches the screenshot: CPU block \"88% (28°C)\", RAM block \"9.6/47.9G\", GPU block \"4% (42°C)\", VRAM block \"1.2/12.0G\", Net up/down separate.\n\nSettings per block: Enabled, Row, ShowIcon, ShowLabel, ShowPercent, ShowValue, ShowTemp, Colors, Label text.\n\nSo MetricKind = Cpu, Gpu, Ram, Vram, NetUp, NetDown, DiskUsed, DiskFree, Battery, CpuTemp? Keep simple: Cpu, Gpu, Ram, Vram, NetUp, NetDown, DiskActivity, DiskFree, Battery. 9 blocks.\n\nDefaults:\nRow1: NetUp, Cpu, Ram\nRow2: NetDown, Gpu, Vram\n\n**Icons**: Use Segoe Fluent Icons / Segoe MDL2 Assets glyphs — present on Win10 & 11. Glyphs:\n- Up arrow: \\uE74A? (Up) `\\uE74A` is \"Up\". Down: `\\uE74B`. Chevron up/down: E70E, E70D.\n- CPU: `\\uE950` (Chip? \"Processor\" maybe E950 is \"Memory\"?). MDL2 glyphs: E950 = \"Chip\"? Let me recall: E7F4 = \"Memory\"? Hmm. Segoe MDL2 list: E950 = \"System\" maybe. Safer: use simple vector Path drawings for CPU chip, GPU card, RAM stick, disk, battery. That's more work but reliable and looks like the screenshot's colored icons.\n\nLet me create small vector icons via Geometry path data:\n- CPU: square with pins (like the blue icon in screenshot).\n- GPU: board shape.\n- RAM: vertical stick with contacts.\n- Disk: circle/HDD rectangle.\n- Net up/down: arrows (simple path).\n- Battery: rectangle with terminal.\n\nI'll draw them at 12x12 in XAML using Path with ScaleTransform if needed. Simplest: use Unicode arrows \"↑\" and \"↓\" as TextBlock (works in any font, matches screenshot). And for CPU/GPU/RAM/VRAM use colored square icons? The screenshot uses a monitor icon for CPU (blue) and a green icon for GPU. I can do small rounded rect colored icon. Hmm, that looks cheap.\n\nUse Segoe MDL2 Assets glyphs with known codepoints:\n- E950 \"Chip\"? Actually Segoe MDL2: E964 = \"Memory\"? Let me recall from MDL2 cheat sheet: \n  - E7F4 = \"PC1\"? \n  - E950 = \"System\"? \n  - EEA0 = \"Processor\"? \n  - E975 = \"Devices2\"?\n  - EA86 = \"Camera\"?\n  - E7F8 = \"Tablet\"?\n  - E88E = \"HardDrive\"? Hmm E88E is \"Save\"? \n\nI don't remember reliably. Segoe Fluent Icons has E950 = \"Chip\"? Risky.\n\nVector paths are deterministic. I'll build a small icon factory in code: each metric's icon drawn as a Path with Data string, colored via brush. Let me write geometry strings:\n\n12x12 viewbox coordinates (0..12):\n- Chip (CPU): `M3,3 H9 V9 H3 Z M1.5,4.5 H3 M1.5,7.5 H3 M9,4.5 H10.5 M9,7.5 H10.5 M4.5,1.5 V3 M7.5,1.5 V3 M4.5,9 V10.5 M7.5,9 V10.5` as stroke geometry. Easier: use rectangle + lines via XAML shapes rather than one Path. In code-behind creating UI, I can add a Canvas with shapes. Or define StrokeGeometry strings and use Path with Stroke.\n\nUse Path with StrokeThickness 1.2, StrokeLineJoin Round, Data e.g.:\n- CPU: `M4,4 H8 V8 H4 Z M2,5.5 H4 M2,6.5 H4 ...` hmm.\n\nAlternative simpler: draw a filled rounded square with the letter? No.\n\nLet me define icons as XAML Data strings (filled shapes look best at 12px):\n- CPU chip: outer square 3..9 (stroke), 4 pins each side (stroke), inner filled square 5..7.\n- GPU: a card: rectangle 1..11 x 3..9 with a fan circle? Simplify: rectangle outline + 2 small circles.\n- RAM: vertical rectangle 4..8 x 1.5..10.5 with 4 contact lines at bottom.\n- Disk: circle with inner dot + arc.\n- Battery: rounded rect 1..10 x 3..9 + terminal 10..11 x 5..7 + fill level.\n- NetUp: arrow up; NetDown: arrow down. Can be text \"↑\"/\"↓\" styled — good.\n\nActually simpler and prettier: use Segoe Fluent Icons but verify glyphs by rendering a test image? I can generate a WPF test window that displays glyphs and screenshot it... too much. Vectors it is, with a fallback of nothing if not defined.\n\nHonestly, the visual fidelity to the screenshot matters less than working readings. I'll implement icons as small Path geometries defined in a static IconFactory, drawn with 2 colors (accent + secondary) — decent.\n\nLet me write geometries carefully in a 16x16 box (easier numbers), then scale to 12 DIP via Viewbox.\n\nCPU (16x16):\nouter rect: M4,4 H12 V12 H4 Z (stroke)\npins: M2,6 H4 M2,8 H4 M2,10 H4 M12,6 H14 M12,8 H14 M12,10 H14 M6,2 V4 M8,2 V4 M10,2 V4 M6,12 V14 M8,12 V14 M10,12 V14\ninner: M6.5,6.5 H9.5 V9.5 H6.5 Z (fill)\n\nGPU:\ncard: M1.5,4 H14.5 V12 H1.5 Z (stroke)\nbracket: M1.5,12 V14 M1.5,14 H5\nfan: circle center (10.5,8) r 2 (stroke) + dot center\nslot: M4,6 H6 M4,8 H6 M4,10 H6? That's more like a card. Good.\n\nRAM:\nstick: M5,3 H11 V13 H5 Z (stroke) with notch: maybe just outline + contacts: M6,13 V14.5 M8,13 V14.5 M10,13 V14.5 and a small line M7,5 H9.\n\nDisk:\ncircle r 5 at (8,8) stroke + inner circle r 1.5 fill + arc? Use ellipse + line from 8,8 to 11.5,5.5.\n\nBattery:\nbody: M1.5,5 H11.5 V11 H1.5 Z (stroke); terminal M11.5,7 H13 V9 H11.5; fill rect M2.5,6 H(2.5+level*8) V10 H2.5 Z.\n\nNetwork arrows: use glyph \"↑\"/\"↓\" in a TextBlock with FontWeight SemiBold. Or vector: up arrow M8,2 L12,7 H9.5 V13 H6.5 V7 H4 Z. Good, filled. Down arrow mirrored.\n\nOK.\n\n**Rendering approach**: In `OverlayWindow`, build two `StackPanel` rows inside a `Border`. On metrics update, instead of rebuilding UI each tick, pre-create a `MetricBlock` UserControl per enabled metric with named TextBlocks; update text/color each tick. Rebuild when settings change.\n\n`MetricBlock : Grid`? Let me make a custom control in code: `MetricView.xaml` with:\n- horizontal StackPanel: [icon][label][value][extra].\n\nSimplify: `MetricView` (UserControl) with a single TextBlock? Rich formatting (label colored differently) needs multiple runs. But we can build an `Inline` composition in a single TextBlock per block: icon is separate, then a TextBlock with Runs: \"RAM \" (colored), \"9.6/47.9G\" (color). That's easy to update: set Run.Text.\n\nSo MetricView layout:\n```\n<StackPanel Orientation=\"Horizontal\" Margin=\"6,0\">\n   <Viewbox Width=\"13\" Height=\"13\"><Path .../></Viewbox>\n   <TextBlock x:Name=\"ValueText\" FontFamily=\"Segoe UI\" FontSize=\"12\" Margin=\"4,0,0,0\"/>\n</StackPanel>\n```\nRuns for label/value/temp set in code.\n\nThen the overlay computes text per metric from snapshot:\n- Cpu: runs: (\"CPU \", labelColor) if ShowLabel; ($\"{usage:0}%\", valueColor); (\" (28°C)\", tempColor) if temp.\n- Ram: label \"RAM\", value \"9.6/47.9G\".\n- NetUp: no label; value \"0.0 Mbps\" with arrow icon.\nAlignment: in screenshot values are right-aligned within columns (\"0.0 Mbps\" aligned), but we can left-align with fixed widths. Use a MinWidth per block? Set `MinWidth` per metric in settings? Simpler: right-align numeric values by giving each block fixed width computed in settings? Hmm. I'll add uniform column widths: Net speed blocks width 82 DIP, CPU/GPU 88, RAM/VRAM 84. Configure via settings \"column spacing\". Since values change (10.0 vs 0.0 Mbps), fixed width prevents jitter. Use `Width` bound to block MinWidth and TextAlignment right? Let me set each MetricView MinWidth = 0 and add right padding; taskbar overlay is left-aligned overall. To avoid jitter, use tabular numbers: `Typography.NumeralAlignment=\"Tabular\"` in WPF (works with Segoe UI? maybe). Also format net speed to 1 decimal fixed width. Fine.\n\n**Background**: `Border` with CornerRadius 4, Background = configurable brush, Padding (6,2). The screenshot shows a block with slightly different shade and no border. On Win11, the taskbar is ~48px tall; the block ~40px (two rows of ~18px). Font 12 → row height 16.\n\n**Positioning math**: overlay width is dynamic (SizeToContent). Position: right edge = taskbar rect right - trayWidth - gap. For taskbar at bottom: x = taskbar.Right - offsetFromRight - width; y = taskbar.Top + (taskbar.Height - height)/2.\n\nFor vertical taskbar (left/right): rotate layout? Complicated. Support: for left/right taskbars, stack rows vertically and place at bottom. I'll implement orientation: if taskbar width < height → vertical → place block rotated? Simplest: keep horizontal layout and center it vertically at bottom of a vertical taskbar, width might exceed taskbar width. Rare case. I'll handle by placing horizontally centered near the bottom for vertical taskbars, and note it. Or hide with message? Let's handle gracefully: for vertical taskbars, place the overlay centered horizontally within taskbar and near bottom; text may overflow. Acceptable for v1 (most users use bottom).\n\nActually I could rotate 90°, ugly. Skip.\n\n**Taskbar detection**:\n- Primary: `FindWindow(\"Shell_TrayWnd\", null)`.\n- Secondary: enumerate windows with class `Shell_SecondaryTrayWnd` (EnumWindows).\n- Get rect. Auto-hide detection: if rect is mostly off screen (rect.Top >= screen.Bottom - 2 for bottom taskbar), hide overlay.\n- Offset from right: settings.TaskbarOffsetX default 250 DIP? Need actual tray width. Better: compute tray rect via FindWindowEx(taskbar, IntPtr.Zero, \"TrayNotifyWnd\", null) → GetWindowRect. On Win11 this may return a tiny/hidden rect. Validate: trayRect width > 40 and within taskbar. Then x = trayRect.Left - gap - width. Else fallback x = taskbar.Right - 300*scale.\n\nLet me test after building.\n\n**Update loop**: `System.Threading.Timer` at 1000ms (configurable 500/1000/2000). Sensor reads on background thread, then Dispatcher.Invoke to update UI. UI is rebuilt only when settings change.\n\nCPU temp via LHM: initialization on background thread; `Computer` with IsCpuEnabled, IsGpuEnabled false (NVML handles GPU), open. Read sensors each tick. LHM requires admin. If fails → CpuTempAvailable=false; show nothing (and settings hint).\n\nLHM API: \n```csharp\nusing LibreHardwareMonitor.Hardware;\nvar computer = new Computer { IsCpuEnabled = true, IsMemoryEnabled = false, ... };\ncomputer.Open();\nforeach (var hw in computer.Hardware) { hw.Update(); foreach (var s in hw.Sensors) if (s.SensorType == SensorType.Temperature && s.Value.HasValue) ... }\n```\nPick sensor with Name \"Core (Tctl/Tdie)\" (AMD) or \"CPU Package\" (Intel) else max.\n\nRAM used: GlobalMemoryStatusEx gives ullTotalPhys, ullAvailPhys. Used = total - avail.\n\nGPU NVML:\n```csharp\n[DllImport(\"nvml.dll\")] ...\n```\nNVML DLL path: `C:\\Windows\\System32\\nvml.dll` exists with NVIDIA driver. Use `nvmlInit_v2` with EntryPoint. Modern drivers expose `nvmlInit_v2`; also `nvmlInit`. Use try both.\n\nStructures:\n```csharp\nstruct nvmlMemory_t { ulong total; ulong free; ulong used; }\nstruct nvmlUtilization_t { uint gpu; uint memory; }\n```\nFunctions: nvmlDeviceGetCount_v2(out uint), nvmlDeviceGetHandleByIndex_v2(uint, out IntPtr), nvmlDeviceGetMemoryInfo(IntPtr, out nvmlMemory_t), nvmlDeviceGetUtilizationRates, nvmlDeviceGetTemperature(IntPtr, int sensorType=0, out uint), nvmlDeviceGetName(IntPtr, StringBuilder, uint) with byte count, nvmlDeviceGetPowerUsage (mW), nvmlDeviceGetFanSpeed.\n\nChoose the discrete NVIDIA GPU (index 0 usually). Read utilization gpu and memory, temp, fan, power.\n\nNetwork: enumerate `NetworkInterface.GetAllNetworkInterfaces()` where OperationalStatus == Up && NetworkInterfaceType != Loopback && != Tunnel && Speed > 0. Sum IPv4Statistics.BytesReceived/Sent. Delta / elapsed → bytes/sec → Mbps = bytes*8/1_000_000.\n\nDisk: Use PDH? Instead use `System.Diagnostics.PerformanceCounter` from NuGet package `System.Diagnostics.PerformanceCounter` 8.0.0. That requires a package. Or PDH P/Invoke. I'll write a small `PdhDiskCounter` using pdh.dll:\n```\nPdhOpenQuery(null, IntPtr.Zero, out query)\nPdhAddEnglishCounter(query, @\"\\PhysicalDisk(_Total)\\% Disk Time\", IntPtr.Zero, out counter)\nPdhCollectQueryData(query)\n... PdhGetFormattedCounterValue(counter, PDH_FMT_DOUBLE, out type, out value)\n```\nPDH_FMT_DOUBLE = 0x00000200. Value can exceed 100 → clamp 0-100. Localized counter names: use PdhAddEnglishCounterW which works on all locales (Vista+). Good, no NuGet.\n\nDisk free: sum DriveInfo.GetDrives() where IsReady && DriveType.Fixed → TotalSize/TotalFreeSpace.\n\nBattery: GetSystemPowerStatus → BatteryLifePercent (255 unknown), ACLineStatus.\n\nPower draw: NVML power for GPU; CPU power needs LHM (package power) — include as part of Cpu block optional \"W\". Skip for v1? They asked \"بطارية / استهلاك الكهرباء\" and selected it. GPU power easy via NVML, CPU package power via LHM when available. I'll add optional `ShowPower` flags on Cpu/Gpu blocks.\n\n**Settings window**: This is a big XAML. Design:\n- Left nav? Keep single scrollable page with sections, 720x680, Windows 11 style (rounded, light/dark). Sections:\n  1. Preview (live mini overlay preview on a fake taskbar strip)\n  2. Blocks (list with enable checkbox, row combo, up/down, icon/label/percent/temp toggles?) — to keep UI manageable: list rows with: checkbox, block name, row selector (1/2), and up/down arrows. Plus per-block detail panel below? Simplify: \"Show label\" global toggle; per-block details only label text? I'll include: blocks list with Enabled + Row + Move up/down; right side \"Block options\" with checkboxes for ShowLabel, ShowIcon, ShowPercent, ShowTemperature, ShowPower, ShowFan + label text box. Bound to selected block.\n  3. Appearance: theme (Auto/Light/Dark), background color + opacity, label/value/temp/warning/critical colors, font family/size, panel corner radius? (maybe), opacity.\n  4. Position: monitor selection (All/Primary/Specific), Offset from right (slider), vertical offset, scale (font size covers).\n  5. Behavior: refresh interval, start with Windows (creates scheduled task), pause when fullscreen? (nice: hide when a fullscreen app/game runs — they'd want that? It's on taskbar so invisible anyway). Add \"Hide when taskbar auto-hidden\" implicit.\n  6. CPU temperature status: shows \"Available / Requires admin\".\n  \n- Save button? Autosave with debounce + Apply live. Use \"Save\" + \"Close\" simpler: settings changes apply immediately on change (live preview) and save to disk with 500ms debounce. Provide \"Reset defaults\" button.\n\nColor picker: text box with hex + a palette of swatches + opacity slider per color? Use WPF ColorDialog? WinForms ColorDialog is available since UseWindowsForms — easiest: button opens ColorDialog. But Win11 styling is meh; acceptable. I'll add small color swatch buttons opening WinForms ColorDialog.\n\n**Data binding**: Implement AppSettings as plain class with INotifyPropertyChanged? That's a lot. Instead, SettingsWindow reads/writes controls and calls `App.Current.ApplySettings(settings)` then `SettingsStore.Save`. Use code-behind event handlers; avoid MVVM boilerplate. Faster and less error-prone.\n\nI need to be careful about scope — this is a big app. Let me write it in a few files with reasonable size (~1500-2000 lines total). Feasible.\n\nLet me start writing.\n\nOrder:\n1. csproj, manifest, App.xaml/cs\n2. Models: MetricKind, BlockSettings, AppSettings, SettingsStore\n3. Services: NativeMethods, MetricsSnapshot, MetricsService, GpuNvml, CpuTemperatureSource, NetworkMeter, DiskMeter, BatteryMeter, TaskbarLocator, StartupManager, ThemeHelper\n4. UI: Icons, MetricView, OverlayWindow, SettingsWindow\n5. Build, run, screenshot, iterate.\n\nSince SDK install runs in background, start writing now.\n\nLet me define settings JSON with defaults.\n\n```csharp\npublic enum MetricKind { Cpu, Gpu, Ram, Vram, NetUp, NetDown, DiskActivity, DiskFree, Battery }\n\npublic sealed class BlockSettings\n{\n    public MetricKind Kind { get; set; }\n    public bool Enabled { get; set; } = true;\n    public int Row { get; set; } = 1;\n    public int Order { get; set; }\n    public string Label { get; set; } = \"\";\n    public bool ShowLabel { get; set; } = true;\n    public bool ShowIcon { get; set; } = true;\n    public string LabelColor { get; set; } = \"#FF1F6FB5\";\n    public string ValueColor { get; set; } = \"#FF1A1A1A\";\n    public string AccentColor { get; set; } = \"#FF2E7D32\";  // icon color\n    public bool ShowPercent { get; set; } = true;\n    public bool ShowTemperature { get; set; } = true;\n    public bool ShowPower { get; set; }\n    public bool ShowFan { get; set; }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, per-block colors complicate settings UI. Simplify: global colors: LabelColor, ValueColor, TemperatureColor, WarningColor, CriticalColor, AccentColor. Per-block color override not needed. In screenshot CPU value is blue/dark, VRAM label green. Global accent can be a single color; fine.\n\nAppSettings:\n```csharp\npublic sealed class AppSettings\n{\n    public int Version { get; set; } = 1;\n    public int RefreshMs { get; set; } = 1000;\n    public string Theme { get; set; } = \"Auto\"; // Auto, Light, Dark\n    public string BackgroundMode { get; set; } = \"Auto\"; // Auto, Solid, Transparent\n    public string BackgroundColor { get; set; } = \"#E6F3F3F3\";\n    public double BackgroundOpacity { get; set; } = 0.9; // applies to solid\n    public string LabelColor ... etc\n    public string FontFamily { get; set; } = \"Segoe UI\";\n    public double FontSize { get; set; } = 12;\n    public double ItemSpacing { get; set; } = 10;\n    public double OffsetFromRight { get; set; } = 250; // DIP gap from taskbar right edge\n    public double OffsetY { get; set; } = 0;\n    public bool ShowOnAllMonitors { get; set; } = false;\n    public string MonitorDeviceName { get; set; } = \"\";  // if not all\n    public bool StartWithWindows { get; set; }\n    public bool StartElevated { get; set; } // scheduled task\n    public double WarningThreshold { get; set; } = 80;\n    public double CriticalThreshold { get; set; } = 92;\n    public List<BlockSettings> Blocks { get; set; } = DefaultBlocks();\n}\n```\nWait: they asked for warning colors, thresholds apply to CPU/RAM/VRAM percentages.\n\nNet speed format: Mbps with 1 decimal; if < 0.05 → \"0.0 Mbps\". Maybe units auto (Kbps for <1)? Screenshot shows Mbps always. Keep Mbps with 1 decimal, but settings \"Auto units\" default false? Keep simple: Mbps.\n\nDisk blocks: DiskActivity shows \"12%\" with icon; DiskFree shows \"54.2/128G\"? Show free/total? Label \"DISK\". Show used/total like RAM. I'll do DiskSpace (used/total) and DiskActivity (%).\n\nBattery: \"94% (AC)\" or \"94% (BAT)\". \n\nNow the overlay window XAML:\n\n```xml\n<Window ... WindowStyle=\"None\" AllowsTransparency=\"True\" Background=\"Transparent\"\n        ShowInTaskbar=\"False\" Topmost=\"True\" ResizeMode=\"NoResize\" SizeToContent=\"WidthAndHeight\"\n        Focusable=\"False\" ShowActivated=\"False\">\n  <Border x:Name=\"Panel\" CornerRadius=\"4\" Padding=\"8,3\" MouseRightButtonUp=\"...\">\n     <StackPanel>\n        <StackPanel x:Name=\"Row1\" Orientation=\"Horizontal\"/>\n        <StackPanel x:Name=\"Row2\" Orientation=\"Horizontal\"/>\n     </StackPanel>\n  </Border>\n</Window>\n```\nSet `WindowChrome`? Not needed.\n\n`AllowsTransparency=True` disables hardware acceleration for the window, but for small overlay fine.\n\nClick-through option: add `WS_EX_NOACTIVATE | WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW` in SourceInitialized. ShowActivated=False so it doesn't steal focus when appearing.\n\nReposition: use `Left/Top` in DIP. Convert screen pixels → DIP using the monitor's DPI. WPF `Window.Left/Top` are in DIP relative to primary monitor's DPI (in PerMonitorV2 WPF, coordinates are per-monitor DIP). For multi-monitor with different DPI, converting from physical pixels requires the target monitor DPI. Use `GetDpiForMonitor` (Shcore) or compute via `VisualTreeHelper.GetDpi` after moving. Simplest robust approach: 