tch \"Show on all taskbars\" + combo. Card \"Placement\": three slider rows with descriptions. Card \"Preview\"? The preview is global at the bottom.\n\n**Startup**: Card \"Windows startup\": two toggle rows with descriptions. Card \"Sensors\": status rows (CPU/GPU) with status text. Card \"Actions\": buttons (Restart as admin, Open settings folder).\n\n**Footer**: preview strip (dark, 64px) with the meter + clock; then a row with \"Reset defaults\" and \"Close\" on the right, and maybe \"Changes save automatically\" on the left.\n\nAlways-visible preview + footer at the bottom of the window; pages scroll above.\n\nThis is a full rewrite of the XAML with mostly the same control names (`ThemeCombo`, `UsePaletteCheck` → maybe becomes a switch but keep the name; `StartWithWindowsCheck` etc.). To minimize code-behind churn, keep the same control types and names where possible:\n- CheckBoxes become styled switches but remain CheckBox controls → code-behind unchanged.\n- ComboBoxes stay; style them minimally (no custom template, just default with padding). Default ComboBox in light theme looks okay-ish (Aero2). To look modern, set `BorderBrush`, `Padding`, `Height=32`, `Background=#F7F8FA`. Template remains default; acceptable.\n- Sliders: default WPF slider looks dated (small thumb). Style them with a custom template: a 4px track + 16px round thumb + accent fill. That's ~25 lines of XAML, worth it for the look.\n- Buttons: flat style with rounded corners (custom template) for secondary buttons, and an accent \"Close\" button? NetSpeedTray has Cancel/Save. Mine auto-saves; use \"Close\" as the primary accent button. \"Reset defaults\" secondary. Also \"Restart as administrator\" secondary.\n- TextBox: styled with padding/rounded border via a template? Default TextBox with a border; set Height=32, Padding, BorderBrush. Default template has square corners; acceptable, or wrap in a rounded Border. Minor. Use the default with a subtle border.\n\nDark sidebar nav: custom Radio